@page "/persistence"
@using System.Text.Json
@implements IDisposable
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IBelemetryConsent Consent

<PageTitle>Offline persistence - bit Belemetry</PageTitle>

<h1>Offline persistence</h1>

<p class="page-intro">
    A train goes into a tunnel, a laptop lid closes mid-export, a collector restarts. Without persistence
    the telemetry of exactly those moments - often the interesting ones - is lost. With it, an export
    request that fails with a network error or a retryable status is kept on the device and sent again
    later: in <code>localStorage</code> in the browser, and in files in Blazor Hybrid and on servers.
</p>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>What is kept, and when</h2>
    <p>
        Persistence works on whole export requests, already serialized, rather than on records: what is
        stored is exactly the body that could not be delivered, so it is sent again unchanged. A request is
        kept when it fails with a network error, or with one of the statuses a receiver uses to say
        "later":
    </p>
    <div class="badge-list">
        <span class="badge">408 Request Timeout</span>
        <span class="badge">429 Too Many Requests</span>
        <span class="badge">439 (Azure daily quota)</span>
        <span class="badge">500</span>
        <span class="badge">502</span>
        <span class="badge">503</span>
        <span class="badge">504</span>
    </div>
    <p>
        Before a request is stored it is first retried in place a few times, with backoff that honours
        <code>Retry-After</code>. Any other status - a 400 for a malformed body, a 401 for a wrong key - is
        a permanent answer, and the request is dropped rather than replayed forever. An Azure Monitor
        <code>206</code> partial success retries only the items the response lists as retryable.
    </p>
    <p>Persistence needs two things at once:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>options.Persistence.Enabled</code> - on by default - which is the operator's switch.</li>
        <li>
            The user's consent for the <code>Persistence</code> feature. Keeping data on someone's device is a
            decision of its own under GDPR, so it is a feature bit of its own, and nothing is written before it
            is granted.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@OptionsCode" Language="C#" Expanded="true" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Where it is kept</h2>
    <div class="doc-table-wrap">
        <table class="doc-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Host</th><th scope="col">Store</th><th scope="col">Size cap</th><th scope="col">Retried</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Browser (every render mode)</td>
                    <td><code>localStorage</code> key <code>bit-belemetry-queue</code></td>
                    <td><code>BrowserMaxBytes</code> (1 MB)</td>
                    <td>Every <code>RetryInterval</code> (1 minute) while the page is open, when the script starts (the next page load), when the browser fires <code>online</code>, and when <code>Persistence</code> is granted</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>WebAssembly .NET pipeline</td>
                    <td>Handed to the script, which stores it in the same <code>localStorage</code> queue</td>
                    <td><code>BrowserMaxBytes</code></td>
                    <td>As above - the script owns the retry, and can also send with <code>keepalive</code> during unload</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Blazor Hybrid, and the server pipeline</td>
                    <td>Files under <code>Directory</code> (default <code>{LocalApplicationData}/bit-belemetry/{service.name}</code>)</td>
                    <td><code>MaxBytes</code> (20 MB)</td>
                    <td>Every <code>RetryInterval</code> (1 minute)</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Past the size cap the <strong>oldest</strong> requests are evicted first, and a request older than
        <code>MaxAge</code> (48 hours) is discarded unsent - telemetry from two days ago rarely answers a
        question anyone is still asking, and a device should not fill up with it. Neither store keeps request
        headers - an API key has no business sitting in <code>localStorage</code> or in a file - so they are
        added again from the exporter's current configuration when a request is resent (a rotated key applies
        to the backlog too). Credentials that expire, such as an Entra
        ID token for Azure Monitor, are never stored on either side: the exporter adds a fresh one at send time.
    </p>
    <p>Each entry of the browser queue has this shape:</p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@QueueShapeCode" Language="JSON" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Look at the queue</h2>
    <p>
        This panel reads <code>localStorage['bit-belemetry-queue']</code> directly. On a healthy connection it
        is empty - nothing failed, so nothing was kept. To fill it, open your browser's developer tools, set the
        <strong>Network</strong> panel to <em>Offline</em>, press <strong>Track an event</strong>, and read the
        queue again. Then go back online: the script hears the <code>online</code> event, resends, and the
        queue empties.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <dl class="kv">
                <dt>Persistence consent</dt>
                <dd><span class="state-pill @(_persistenceGranted is true ? "on" : null)">@(_persistenceGranted switch { true => "granted", false => "not granted", null => "unknown" })</span></dd>
                <dt>bit-belemetry-queue</dt>
                <dd>
                    @if (_queueRead is false)
                    {
                        <span>not read yet</span>
                    }
                    else if (_queueEntries is null)
                    {
                        <span class="state-pill">empty</span>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span class="state-pill on">@_queueEntries request(s), @FormatBytes(_queueBytes)</span>
                    }
                </dd>
                @if (_queueExporters.Length > 0)
                {
                    <dt>Waiting for</dt>
                    <dd>@string.Join(", ", _queueExporters)</dd>
                }
            </dl>
            <div class="demo-actions">
                <button type="button" class="btn-bl" disabled="@(RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false)" @onclick="ReadQueueAsync">Read the queue</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn-bl-subtle" disabled="@(RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false)" @onclick="TrackEventAsync">Track an event</button>
                @if (_persistenceGranted is true)
                {
                    <button type="button" class="btn-bl btn-bl-danger" disabled="@(RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false)" @onclick="RevokeAsync">Revoke Persistence</button>
                }
                else
                {
                    <button type="button" class="btn-bl-subtle" disabled="@(RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false)" @onclick="GrantAsync">Grant Persistence</button>
                }
            </div>
            @if (_message is not null)
            {
                <p class="demo-label">@_message</p>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            <strong>Track an event</strong> needs the <code>Events</code> feature granted, and the queue only
            fills with <code>Persistence</code> granted. Revoking <code>Persistence</code> clears the queue at once:
            read it again after pressing <strong>Revoke Persistence</strong> while it holds something.
        </p>
    </div>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@ReadQueueCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Revoking clears it</h2>
    <p>
        Withdrawing consent has to reach data already stored, not only data still to come. Revoking
        <code>Persistence</code> - from the banner, from <code>IBelemetryConsent.RevokeAsync</code> or from
        <code>BitBelemetry.setConsent</code> in the browser - removes the <code>bit-belemetry-queue</code> key in the
        browser and deletes the stored files on the .NET side. Revoking any <em>other</em> feature purges that
        feature's records still queued in memory, so nothing it collected leaves the device afterwards.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@RevokeCode" Language="Razor" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>Hybrid apps</h2>
    <p>
        In MAUI, WPF and WinForms the browser script relays its records to .NET, and the .NET pipeline exports
        everything - so the file store is the one that matters. A phone is suspended long before it loses its
        files, so flush when the app goes to the background; what does not get through is stored and retried on
        the next <code>RetryInterval</code> after the app resumes.
    </p>
    <CodeSnippet Code="@HybridCode" Language="C#" />
</div>

<div class="demo-section">
    <h2>API</h2>
    <ApiTable TypeName="BelemetryPersistenceOptions" />
</div>

@code {
    private const string OptionsCode = """
        builder.Services.AddBitBelemetryServices(options =>
        {
            options.Persistence.Enabled = true;                          // the default; consent still applies
            options.Persistence.BrowserMaxBytes = 1024 * 1024;           // localStorage is small: 1 MB
            options.Persistence.MaxBytes = 20 * 1024 * 1024;             // the file store (Hybrid, server): 20 MB
            options.Persistence.MaxAge = TimeSpan.FromHours(48);         // discarded unsent after this
            options.Persistence.RetryInterval = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1); // how often stored requests are retried (both sides)
            // options.Persistence.Directory = "...";                    // defaults to LocalApplicationData
        });
        """;

    private const string QueueShapeCode = """
        [
          {
            "id": "a3f1c2...",
            "exporter": "otlp",
            "url": "https://otel.example.com/v1/logs",
            "contentType": "application/x-protobuf",
            "body": "<base64>",
            "time": 1760000000000
          }
        ]
        """;

    private const string ReadQueueCode = """
        @inject IJSRuntime JS

        @code {
            private async Task ReadQueueAsync()
            {
                // The queue is a plain localStorage entry, so any page can look at it.
                var json = await JS.InvokeAsync<string?>("localStorage.getItem", "bit-belemetry-queue");
                using var queue = json is null ? null : JsonDocument.Parse(json);
                var pending = queue?.RootElement.GetArrayLength() ?? 0;
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string RevokeCode = """
        @inject IBelemetryConsent Consent

        <button @onclick="() => Consent.RevokeAsync(BelemetryFeatures.Persistence)">
            Do not keep telemetry on this device
        </button>
        """;

    private const string HybridCode = """
        // App.xaml.cs (MAUI), with the app's IServiceProvider injected into its constructor as _services
        protected override async void OnSleep()
        {
            base.OnSleep();

            try
            {
                // Async work does not survive suspension: hand the queues over while the app still runs.
                // IBelemetry is scoped, so it is resolved from a scope of its own.
                await using var scope = _services.CreateAsyncScope();
                await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IBelemetry>().FlushAsync();
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                // async void: an exception here would crash the app on its way to the background.
                System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine($"Flushing telemetry failed: {ex}");
            }
        }
        """;

    private bool? _persistenceGranted;
    private bool _queueRead;
    private int? _queueEntries;
    private long _queueBytes;
    private string[] _queueExporters = [];
    private string? _message;

    // The reader usually decides on the banner while this page is open, so the state shown here follows every
    // consent change, not only the ones made with this page's own buttons.
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Consent.Changed += OnConsentChanged;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender is false || RendererInfo.IsInteractive is false) return;

        await RefreshAsync();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private void OnConsentChanged(object? sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        _persistenceGranted = Consent.IsGranted(BelemetryFeatures.Persistence);
        _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    public void Dispose() => Consent.Changed -= OnConsentChanged;

    private async Task RefreshAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await Consent.RefreshAsync();
            _persistenceGranted = Consent.IsGranted(BelemetryFeatures.Persistence);
        }
        catch
        {
            // Consent that cannot be read is shown as unknown; the queue panel still works.
            _persistenceGranted = null;
        }
    }

    private async Task ReadQueueAsync()
    {
        await RefreshAsync();

        try
        {
            var json = await JS.InvokeAsync<string?>("localStorage.getItem", "bit-belemetry-queue");
            _queueRead = true;
            _queueEntries = null;
            _queueBytes = 0;
            _queueExporters = [];

            if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(json)) return;

            using var queue = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
            if (queue.RootElement.ValueKind is not JsonValueKind.Array || queue.RootElement.GetArrayLength() == 0) return;

            _queueEntries = queue.RootElement.GetArrayLength();
            _queueBytes = System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetByteCount(json);
            _queueExporters = [.. queue.RootElement.EnumerateArray()
                                                    .Select(e => e.TryGetProperty("exporter", out var x) ? x.GetString() : null)
                                                    .OfType<string>()
                                                    .Distinct()];
            _message = null;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Storage blocked by the browser (private mode, site data disabled) is a reading, not a crash.
            _message = $"The queue could not be read: {ex.Message}";
        }
    }

    private async Task TrackEventAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("BitBelemetry.trackEvent", "demo.persistence", new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["demo.page"] = "persistence" });
            // flush() resolves once the batch is handed to the transport - delivered, or stored if it failed.
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("BitBelemetry.flush");
            _message = "Event tracked and flushed. Read the queue to see whether it was kept.";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _message = $"The browser script did not answer: {ex.Message}";
        }
    }

    private async Task GrantAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await Consent.GrantAsync(BelemetryFeatures.Persistence);
            _message = "Persistence granted: failed exports are now kept on this device.";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _message = $"Consent could not be changed: {ex.Message}";
        }

        await RefreshAsync();
    }

    private async Task RevokeAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await Consent.RevokeAsync(BelemetryFeatures.Persistence);
            _message = "Persistence revoked: the stored queue was cleared.";
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _message = $"Consent could not be changed: {ex.Message}";
        }

        await RefreshAsync();
        await ReadQueueAsync();
    }

    private static string FormatBytes(long bytes)
        => bytes < 1024 ? $"{bytes} B" : $"{bytes / 1024.0:0.#} KB";
}
